<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- lane-liveness (#1178, unit 3): the SCHEDULED lane-liveness report. The
         owner requirement is "a way to check which lanes are stuck, independent
         of the orchestrator being dead" — a pull tool is silent in exactly that
         scenario, so this job runs on a timer and escalates a dead lane to a
         deduped GitHub issue with no orchestrator alive.

         WHAT IT DOES: enumerates the fleet's lanes from the durable cmux hook
         store (one lane per workspace, newest non-child record), so a lane whose
         pi process is GONE is still enumerated — the population is not derived
         from the live process table — and asks the ONE shared identity predicate
         (scripts/lib/pid-identity.sh, the same library the kill-path reaper
         sources) for a verdict. It never uses a bare kill(0) presence check.
         It also never calls the `cmux` CLI: `cmux workspace list` refuses
         launchd callers with "Access denied - only processes started inside
         cmux can connect" (measured by running this very job), so every runtime
         input is a FILE — the hook store, the session JSONLs, `ps`.

         SCHEDULE: every 30 min. Measured cost on this host: ~27 lanes →
         ~60 forks of a full `ps` table → ~85 s per pass (well under the
         interval, and the box's other load dominates). 30 min rather than
         hourly because the incident this closes was a lane dead for FIVE HOURS;
         the first pass after a lane dies files the issue.

         TCC (#427/#432): launchd cannot read ~/Documents, so every runtime path
         is outside that wall —
           * the driver + its classifier
                                            {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/fleet/
             (pi-bootstrap/setup.sh scripts/fleet farm; real copies, not symlinks)
           * the probe library
                                            {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/lib/
             (the #1178 lib farm, unit 2)
           * store / sessions / log         ~/.cmuxterm, ~/.pi/agent/state/
         This job used to be farmed into ~/.pi/agent/tools/fleet/, which is Pi's
         RETIRED custom-tools namespace: Pi's startup check
         (dist/migrations.js checkDeprecatedExtensionDirs) rejects any entry
         there that is not fd/rg, and its deprecation prompt is an untimed
         keypress wait — so the misplaced farm blocked every fresh
         interactive Pi boot (#1277). scripts/fleet/ is outside that scan.
         PI_PID_IDENTITY_LIB is pinned explicitly rather than left to the
         driver's relative resolution, so the TCC safety is auditable in the
         installed plist: liveness.lib_path() would otherwise resolve
         <here>/../../scripts/lib/pid-identity.sh, which farmed at
         ~/.pi/agent/scripts/fleet/ is exactly the PINNED
         ~/.pi/agent/scripts/lib/pid-identity.sh — the farmed fleet/ sits
         visibly beside the farmed lib/, so the pin and the fallback agree by
         construction rather than by coincidence.

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: the pass appends a capped footer
         to ~/.pi/agent/state/lane-liveness.log (world-writable /tmp siblings are
         a symlink-truncation surface + an unbounded-launchd-capture twin — the
         same reasoning as the reaper's plist).
         Rendered by scripts/install-launchd.sh (#304) — placeholders only. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.eldato.lane-liveness</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
        <string>python3</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/fleet/lane_liveness.py</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>PI_PID_IDENTITY_LIB</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/lib/pid-identity.sh</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>1800</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
